Tech Change Management
If a bouncer in ballarat nite club had a deny list it would have 120k names on it so a allow list would work better 
Why an allow list is safer (the real reason). A deny list only lists the known bad stuff, which is why it’s dangerous:
anything new gets straight through. A brand new piece of malware isn’t on anyone’s banned list yet, so the deny-list
bouncer waves it in. The allow-list bouncer doesn’t care whether you’re a known troublemaker. If you’re not on the guest list,
you’re not coming in, full stop. That’s why allow lists stop zero-day malware (new threats
nobody has seen yet) and deny lists don’t. The trade-off is that allow lists are more work to manage, 
which is why IT staff who understand the risks surrounding security and researchers who need to use so many sites and resources to get their often get looser rules,

Maintenance windows: Friday night or Saturday at ANZ, the quiet times when a change affects the fewest people.
Staged (phased) rollout: updating a few machines at a time on a 24/7 system instead of everything at once. If something breaks, only a small part is affected.
Redundancy: keeping spare machines so work never stops. In big systems this becomes high availability, where a backup server takes over automatically while the main one is restarted.
Standard image (golden image): every ANZ computer built from the same base image, so any machine can replace any other.
Access tied to the user, not the device: that’s very zero trust,
Mid-task: if the payroll machine restarted halfway through a pay run, you could end up with half-written data, so some staff paid twice and some not at all. That’s called data corruption.
Service restart vs system restart: sometimes you only need to restart one program (a service), not the whole computer. Less disruption, but you need to know which one.
Legacy apps: old systems may not come back cleanly after a restart this is what happened at MIA and i expected this and raised concerns if a change management plan was in place it would have not been effected. 


