Asymmetric algorithms like RSA can only encrypt a small amount of data at a time, smaller than the key itself. So to encrypt a big file with RSA alone, you really would have to chop it into tiny pieces and encrypt each one separately. That’s your “one van per item”: slow, and “costly” in computing terms (CPU time, battery, server load).

So the full picture with my analogy: Move house in one quick move using hybrid. 

Asymmetric only: one van per item. It works, but it’s absurdly slow and expensive. If I have a lot of items it would be millions of van trips time consuming, processor consuming or in this case mechanically that trucks doing a lot of clicks. imagine the petrol bill yikes. 
Hybrid: you use a secure courier once to deliver the key to a big removal truck (asymmetric sends the session key), then load everything into the truck and move it in one go (symmetric/AES encrypts the data).

