Encrypt with your public key → only you can read it.

Encrypting for someone → use their public key (only their private key decrypts). 
Signing for someone → use your private key (only your public key verifies). 
The two operations are mirror images. One protects confidentiality (only the intended recipient can read), the other protects authenticity + integrity + non-repudiation (anyone can verify, but only the signer could have created it).

One thing CompTIA loves to trip you on: a digital signature does not provide confidentiality.  The message can be sent in the clear and still be signed — anyone can read it, but they can't forge or alter it without detection.


Block Chains 1.4

if you were a bank with a single central database, what would an attacker need to do to change a record? And how does that compare with blockchain?
in a central database to change a record you would only need to break in to that one database like on a holiday when no one is looking on a block chain you would have to get access to every computer that has the block chain records and you would have to be quick about it before some one noticed it could be 1 milion computers and so you would need a long holiday with no one looking

You don’t need every computer, just the majority. If an attacker controls more than half the network’s computing power, they can push their version of the ledger as the “truth”. This is called a 51% attack. For large blockchains like Bitcoin it’s wildly expensive, which is the protection. Smaller blockchains with fewer participants have actually been hit by this, so size matters.
Changing one record also means redoing every block after it. Change letter 3 and you have to recalculate the stamps on 4, 5, 6 and onwards, faster than the honest network keeps adding new ones. With proof of work, each stamp is deliberately slow to make, so you’re racing a million people who have a head start.

 central-database comparison is the key exam point: a central database has a single point of failure; blockchain removes it by spreading trust across the network.


